[resource]
script = ExtResource("1_s")
id = "extraction"
display_name = "Turning for home"
title = "Turning for home"
category = "The Road"
order = 14
image = "res://art/guide/extraction.png"
body = "A road does not have to be finished in one sitting. At any fork past the first, the party may turn for home instead of choosing a way on: the run ends as a return rather than a fall, the Marks are paid in full, and the front is banked — the wall, every tower, its level, its path and how badly it is damaged.

Leaving is not instant. The road behind you closes: bodies keep coming, harder as the beast pulls away, and you hold the gate with the board you actually built. The line at the top of the screen counts the road you have left to hold.

What that fight costs is the state of the fortress you take with you. The front is photographed when it ends, not when you press the card, so the wall and every tower come home in whatever condition you left them in.

What it cannot cost you is the journey. The gate cannot fall during a withdrawal and no Wound can end the run there — a party that turns for home always reaches home. Only the condition of what they carry is in question.

Next time, the Hold offers to resume that front rather than starting again at the first act. Everything that lives and grows is laid fresh: the wildlife, the foliage, the weather, whatever was dropped on the ground. The road is alive again; only what you built is where you left it.

A fortification comes back as hurt as it was left. Mending it is the Quartermaster's business, not the road's — leaving the moment anything is scratched would make attrition meaningless.

Pushing past a fork without banking builds momentum, which pays a little more of what the road is hiding: more from every kill and a faster trickle. It buys nothing that reaches a fight. That is the whole trade — bank a front and keep it, or walk on and be paid for the risk.

A wipe never clears the front. Everything before your last extraction is kept; everything since it is what you lose."
